The Stack 3 order is cache-hit bound and effectively noise; its member column is the PHP-sensitive number. The Stack 1/2 order changes slightly on Host B (8.2 leads there).

[HEADING=3]What the numbers mean[/HEADING]

[LIST]
[*][B]Page cache is the whole game.[/B] Stack 3 serves guest pages [B]88× (Host B)[/B] to [B]180× (Host A)[/B] faster than Valkey-only. It is also [B]175× / 82×[/B] faster than the plain stack.
[*][B]Valkey alone does not help XenForo on a single host.[/B] Removing the page cache and adding Valkey costs [B]2–7 %[/B] on guest requests and [B]9–14 %[/B] on member pages. On a separate host it may help; locally MariaDB is already fast and the cache adds serialization + network hops.
[*][B]Anubis is a bot-filter tax, not a performance feature.[/B] It keeps [B]8–10×[/B] the plain stack's guest throughput because the page cache is still behind it, but it removes ~90–94 % of the cached-page ceiling and ~17–19 % of dynamic throughput.
[*][B]Logged-in requests always bypass the FastCGI guest cache.[/B] XenForo's guest-cache map is cookie-gated, so the two member rows are the same with page cache on or off; they measure the cache-bypass path.
[*][B]phpredis vs Credis made no material difference[/B] on PHP 8.5: 698/614 req/s with phpredis vs 697/616 with Credis.
[/LIST]


